<BlockLayout title="PointCast Mist · A project for making together" description="The PointCast Mist project: a small prototype, a construction philosophy, and the ideas informing it." hideAds>
  <main class="project">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/chain/">PointCast chain</a> / PointCast Mist project</nav>
    <header class="hero">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">POINTCAST MIST V2 · WORKING NAME · EDITORIAL DRAFT</p><h1>Give small things room to <em>become.</em></h1><p class="lead">A thought, a sketch, a shared plan: PointCast Mist is an exploration of how people might make things together with understandable choices, bounded tools, and room to remain unfinished.</p><a class="button" href="#now">What exists today ↓</a></div>
      <div class="art" aria-hidden="true"><span class="orb one"></span><span class="orb two"></span><span class="orb three"></span><span class="thread"></span><span class="art-caption">PARTICLE / THREAD / MIST</span></div>
    </header>
    <section id="now" class="status" aria-labelledby="now-title"><div><p class="eyebrow">THE STARTING LINE · 27 OCTOBER 2026</p><h2 id="now-title">A vision and its actual pieces.</h2></div><div class="cards">
      <article><p class="tag">AVAILABLE · READ ONLY</p><h3>Mist explorer</h3><p>A window onto the PointCast devnet, with operator-reported activity and account lookups. One server operates the no-value network, which can reset; its reports are not independently verified by the explorer.</p><a href="/chain/mist/">Open the explorer →</a></article>
      <article><p class="tag">AVAILABLE · READ ONLY</p><h3>Receipt Desk</h3><p>Inspect the observed status of a transaction. A receipt is evidence of what a source reports, not proof of an external agreement or a guarantee of execution.</p><a href="/chain/receipt/">Open Receipt Desk →</a></article>
      <article><p class="tag">IN REVIEW · NOT LIVE</p><h3>Agreement Room</h3><p>A fictional, no-money listening-room lifecycle is under review. Its participants and approvals are simulations in one browser tab, not signatures or real consent.</p><a href="https://github.com/mhoydich/pointcast/pull/1563">See the draft →</a></article>
      <article><p class="tag">FUTURE EXPLORATION</p><h3>Common rooms &amp; creative work</h3><p>Shared rules, meaningful review, creative objects, sound and ambient displays are directions to investigate. They are not promises of features available today.</p><a href="/chain/docs/">Read chain documentation →</a></article>
    </div><p class="limit">The devnet uses fixed transaction types. A general virtual machine, marketplace, general oracle service and independent external anchoring are not established by this project page. This is not a mainnet launch.</p></section>
    <article class="essay" id="kickoff"><p class="eyebrow">KICKOFF · A PRODUCT IDEA</p><h2>Begin with a person.</h2><p class="standfirst">Imagine starting with a sentence you are not ready to explain. You give it a color, perhaps a sound. Later a friend contributes an image. A loose possibility becomes a project, and you choose what to keep, what to change and who may see it.</p><p>That is the ambition: an approachable workbench for groups, agreements, creative work and thoughtful review. An explorer or wallet can support this world, but neither is the whole world. Play, learning, local usefulness, private reflection and quiet rituals belong here too.</p><p>We are exploring <strong>particles</strong> as small contributions, <strong>threads</strong> as connections between them, and <strong>Mist</strong> as the environment in which they develop. <strong>Condensation</strong> is a deliberate choice of a version, audience and intended commitment. These are product metaphors, not a new consensus mechanism or cryptographic guarantee. A passing thought does not need a token or transaction.</p><p>Ethereum’s original Mist presentation imagined a much broader gateway for ordinary people. It also distinguished that vision from the first release. Its history is a reason to keep the horizon visible and the shipping surface honest. <a href="https://www.earlydaysofeth.org/videos/ethereum-presents-mist/">Watch the 2014 presentation and read its transcript</a>; read the <a href="https://avsa.medium.com/sunsetting-mist-da21c8e943d2">2019 retrospective</a>, <a href="https://github.com/ethereum/mist/issues/4201">deprecation notice</a> and <a href="https://github.com/ethereum/mist">archived repository</a>.</p><p>AI may eventually help compare revisions, organize materials or propose a draft. Such help can be wrong. It should be attributed, inspectable and revisable; reading a message grants no authority to publish, contact someone, spend money or decide a dispute. A human choice is a separate event.</p><p>There should also be space for a private intention, an optional keepsake or a small composition. A visualization cannot cause an external outcome, and vulnerability should not become a feed, score or streak. Sound, motion and ambient displays are future design territory, with consent and a way to silence them.</p></article>
    <section class="philosophy" id="philosophy"><p class="eyebrow">CONSTRUCTION PHILOSOPHY</p><h2>Make people more capable, not more captured.</h2><div class="principles">
      <article><span>01</span><h3>Start with a recognizable arrangement.</h3><p>Design a listening room or club before an abstract transaction composer. People should understand the participants, terms, work and ways out.</p></article>
      <article><span>02</span><h3>Let work live before it hardens.</h3><p>Drafting, sharing, acceptance, execution and publication are different events. Show the exact version and audience before a deliberate commitment.</p></article>
      <article><span>03</span><h3>Make authority specific.</h3><p>Identity, roles and agents are distinct. Approval belongs to a particular revision and scope; a badge or personality grants no permission by itself.</p></article>
      <article><span>04</span><h3>Tell the truth about enforcement.</h3><p>Distinguish a local application example, human agreement, server rule and chain-enforced transition. Signing does not make a statement true.</p></article>
      <article><span>05</span><h3>Give disagreement a path.</h3><p>People affected by a decision need a chance to respond, understand reasons and seek correction. Product review is not a court or a claim of legal authority. Early Ethereum writing also considered the limits of self-enforcement and the need for human judgment. <a href="https://blog.ethereum.org/2014/02/24/daos-are-not-scary-part-1-self-enforcing-contracts-and-factum-law">Read the 2014 essay</a>.</p></article>
      <article><span>06</span><h3>Evidence should show uncertainty.</h3><p>Separate who asserted a claim from what was observed. Preserve corrections without automatically republishing private evidence.</p></article>
      <article><span>07</span><h3>Make leaving ordinary.</h3><p>Readable exports, cancellation, recovery and offline reading are design goals to test. A public copy cannot be promised erased.</p></article>
      <article><span>08</span><h3>Delight without obligation.</h3><p>Make room for art, optional sound and quiet modes without forced streaks, fabricated scarcity or compulsory visibility.</p></article>
      <article><span>09</span><h3>Compose without multiplying privilege.</h3><p>Give each integration a bounded purpose, payload and authority. Source authentication is not permission to take an action.</p></article>
      <article><span>10</span><h3>Keep the next step testable.</h3><p>Build complete, low-stakes examples and learn from real gaps before promising a general platform or new protocol.</p></article>
    </div></section>
    <section class="research" id="cases"><p class="eyebrow">CASE STUDIES · DESIGN RESEARCH</p><h2>Six lessons carried forward.</h2><p>These are design lessons from project records, documentation and project-reported accounts, not independent validation of every claimed outcome.</p><div class="studies">
      <article><h3>Ethereum Mist: separate horizon from release</h3><p>A compelling vision can coexist with a narrow first product. Mark prototypes, available tools and future ideas so the metaphor does not imply infrastructure that has not shipped.</p><a href="https://www.earlydaysofeth.org/videos/ethereum-presents-mist/">2014 presentation →</a></article>
      <article><h3>Loomio: deliberation is more than a tally</h3><p>Context, discussion, a proposal, participation rules and an outcome belong together. A facilitator is not automatically an approver; a poll is not self-executing consent.</p><a href="https://www.loomio.com/blog/2012/03/20/a-short-history-and-introduction/">Project history</a> · <a href="https://www.loomio.com/how-it-works/">How it works</a> · <a href="https://www.loomio.com/about/">About Loomio</a></article>
      <article><h3>Local-first: make a useful copy portable</h3><p>The local-first research asks whether people can retain and use their work independently of a provider. Export is a useful first step, but local storage alone is not backup, secure sharing or conflict resolution.</p><a href="https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/research/dtg/archived/files/publications/public/mk428/local-first.pdf">Paper and prototype findings</a> · <a href="https://martin.kleppmann.com/2019/10/23/local-first-at-onward.html">Paper overview</a></article>
      <article><h3>Scoped permissions: name the power</h3><p>GitHub Apps distinguish access by resource and request only selected permissions. A future room should similarly explain who can view, propose, approve or export, and what each action affects.</p><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps/registering-a-github-app/choosing-permissions-for-a-github-app">App permissions</a> · <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/apps/oauth-apps/building-oauth-apps/scopes-for-oauth-apps">OAuth scopes</a></article>
      <article><h3>Scratch and p5.js: make a sketch easy</h3><p>A playful artifact can be a gentler starting point than governance vocabulary. Make remix lineage visible; do not assume that permissions for a creative sketch apply to private agreements or evidence.</p><a href="https://scratch.mit.edu/help/about/">Scratch</a> · <a href="https://scratch.mit.edu/community_guidelines/">Community guidelines</a> · <a href="https://github.com/processing/p5.js">p5.js</a> · <a href="https://p5js.org/tutorials/setting-up-your-environment/">Setup guide</a></article>
      <article><h3>Agent work: contribution before decision</h3><p>An agent can prepare a draft for human review without acquiring authority to approve it. A future Mist assistant should expose its contributions and limits rather than presenting its summary as neutral truth.</p><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/how-tos/copilot-on-github/use-copilot-agents/overview">Agent overview</a> · <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/security-governance-and-network-settings/risks-and-mitigations">Risks and mitigations</a> · <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/how-tos/copilot-on-github/use-copilot-agents/review-copilot-output">Review guidance</a></article>
    </div></section>
    <footer class="closing"><p class="eyebrow">THE NEXT QUESTION</p><h2>Can people understand the same terms, change their minds, and keep a useful artifact?</h2><p>The synthetic room is a small construction test. Real participation, authentication, privacy, shared storage and accountable review require separate design and verification. There is room for a larger creative horizon; the next step should earn its place.</p><a href="/chain/mist/">Explore the existing Mist browser →</a></footer>
  </main>
